course: history
lesson: 61
title: How we got here: the big steps on one long timeline
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Leo, in his strategy game: «lol my tribe invented farming on turn 5, ez». His big sister: «Turn 5? Bruv, real people waited about 290,000 years.» (Bruv is London slang for brother or mate.)
explain: What really happened: people like us have lived for about 300,000 years, and farming began only about 12,000 years ago. For almost all of human time everyone hunted animals and gathered plants, nuts and berries.
explain: Miss Okafor squeezes all human time into one school day from midnight to midnight: farming starts a bit before 11 at night, writing at about 11:35, printing in Europe under three minutes before midnight, and electric light at home in the very last minute.
explain: Big steps rarely have one inventor or one switch day. Nobody invented fire, farming began in several lands on its own, and the Stone, Bronze and Iron Ages are labels historians made, not days when everyone upgraded together like in a game.
explain: And history does not only go up. Ethan's mate says one caveman rubbed two sticks and boom, fire; Ethan says «sus», meaning suspicious. Danylo's cousin says every age beats the last; Danylo says «cap», meaning that is a lie. Both are right to doubt.
fact: People like us have lived for about 300,000 years; farming began only about 12,000 years ago, first in the Middle East.
fact: If all human time were one day, writing would appear at about 11:35 at night and electric light at home in the last minute.
fact: Homes got telephones during the 1900s and TV mostly from the 1950s; the World Wide Web was made in 1991, and most homes in rich countries got the internet around 2000.
fact: Nobody invented fire: it existed in lightning and wildfires; earlier kinds of humans learned to keep it maybe a million years ago, and regular campfires go back about 400,000 years.
fact: Nobody knows exactly when people began to talk; many scientists think full language is at least 100,000 years old, and it is still argued about.
fact: The first farmers were often shorter, had worse teeth and caught more illnesses than hunters, but farming fed many more people from the same land.
fact: Farming began separately in several places: wheat in the Middle East, rice in China, maize in Mexico, potatoes in the Andes, bananas and taro in New Guinea, sorghum in Africa.
fact: Bronze came into use in the Middle East about 5,000 years ago and iron about 3,200 years ago, but at very different times elsewhere; some peoples went straight from stone to iron.
fact: Our year numbers count from the birth of Jesus, worked out by a monk called Dionysius around the year 525; years before it count backwards, and BCE and CE mean the same as BC and AD.
fact: Chinese, Islamic and Jewish calendars count years differently, and they are just as real.
fact: Earth is about 4.5 billion years old and the dinosaurs died out 66 million years ago, so no human ever met a dinosaur.
fact: About 12,000 years ago there were only a few million people; about one billion by 1800 and more than eight billion today.
fact: Progress is not a straight climb: after the western Roman Empire broke up, towns in western Europe shrank for centuries, while cities in China and the Islamic world grew.
exercise: How long have people like us lived on Earth? | About 300,000 years | About 6,000 years | About 2,000 years | About 4.5 billion years
exercise: When did farming begin? | About 12,000 years ago | About 300,000 years ago | About 2,000 years ago | About 500 years ago
exercise: How did people get food for almost all of human time? | By hunting and gathering | By farming wheat | By buying it at markets | By fishing from big ships
exercise: If all human time were one day, when would writing appear? | At about 11:35 at night | At about 6 in the morning | At midday | At about 3 in the afternoon
exercise: When was the World Wide Web made? | In 1991 | In about 1950 | In about 1900 | In the year 1066
exercise: Who invented fire? | Nobody; people learned to keep fire from nature | One clever caveman rubbing two sticks together | The ancient Greeks, who gave it to the rest of the world | The first farmers in Egypt, to cook their bread
exercise: How long ago do regular campfires go back? | About 400,000 years | About 4,000 years | About 40 years | About 40 million years
exercise: When did people first start talking? | Nobody knows exactly; probably over 100,000 years ago | Exactly 5,000 years ago, in the first cities of Sumer | When writing was invented, since words need letters | In the year 1 AD, when the calendar began
exercise: What happened to many people when they first switched to farming? | They got shorter and caught more illnesses | They all grew much taller and lived much longer | They stopped getting ill because bread is healthy | They worked fewer hours than hunters
exercise: Where did farming begin? | In several places separately | Only in Mesopotamia | Only in Egypt | Only in Europe
exercise: Which crop was first farmed in Mexico? | Maize | Long-grain rice | Winter wheat | Potatoes
exercise: What are the Stone, Bronze and Iron Ages? | Labels historians use, at different times in different places | Days when the whole world switched to a new metal at once | Laws made by ancient kings about which metals to use | Ages that began and ended at the same time everywhere
exercise: Who worked out our way of numbering years? | A monk called Dionysius | Julius Caesar | The first pharaoh | Isaac Newton
exercise: Which statement about calendars is true? | Chinese, Islamic and Jewish calendars count years differently | Every country on Earth has always counted the years in AD | Only one calendar is real and all the others are mistakes | Year numbers were set by nature, so all calendars agree
exercise: When did the dinosaurs die out? | About 66 million years ago | About 10,000 years ago | About 300,000 years ago | About 2,000 years ago
exercise: About how many people lived in the world in 1800? | About one billion | About eight billion | About a thousand | About a hundred million billion
exercise: What happened to towns in western Europe after the western Roman Empire broke up? | Many shrank for centuries | They doubled in size every year | They got electric street lights | Nothing in them changed at all
test: lol my tribe got farming on turn 5, real life is the same innit | No, real people hunted and gathered for nearly 300,000 years first | Yeah, farming came pretty much right after people did, like day one | Yeah, and the wheel came on turn 1 too, that's just how it went | Nah, farming only came about 500 years ago with the Romans
test: Ethan's mate swears one caveman rubbed two sticks and boom, fire. Sus? | Yes, many groups slowly learned to keep and use fire | No, legit, his name is written down in old books | No, it's true, but it was a Roman, not a caveman | Yes, coz fire was really invented by the first farmers
test: A cartoon shows cavemen riding dinosaurs. What is wrong? | Millions of years separate the dinosaurs and people | Nothing at all: people and dinosaurs lived together | Only the small dinosaurs were really ridden by people | The cavemen should be riding the flying dinosaurs
test: Zara asks: so one day everyone switched to bronze? | No, metals came at different times in different places | Yes, on a day that every king in the world agreed on | Yes, all at once, everywhere, in the year 1000 AD | No, bronze came after iron everywhere in the world
test: «Why do the old years count down, innit weird?» asks Elif. Why? | Years before the birth of Jesus are counted backwards | Because the ancient people just counted their years wrongly | Because time really did run backwards in those days | Because the old kings liked having bigger numbers
test: Grandad Bill says he had no internet as a kid. So was he a knight? | No, he had TV, cars and planes, just no internet | Yes, he lived in a castle | Yes, the olden days had no machines | No, he had smartphones too
test: Ruby sees farmers' skeletons are shorter than hunters'. What does this show? | A big step can help the group and be hard on each person | Farming made everyone healthier, taller and stronger at once | Hunters were taller because they ate only meat and sweets | Farmers were a different animal from the hunters
test: Kai's friend says everyone copied farming from Mesopotamia. What would Kai say? | Nah, at least half a dozen peoples started farming on their own | Correct, nobody else on Earth ever thought of planting seeds | Nah, farming came first from Australia and spread from there | Nah, everyone copied farming from the Romans, not Mesopotamia
test: What would have happened if farming had never begun? | Far fewer people, and no big towns or cities | Exactly the same number of cities as today | More people than today, all of them hunters | Dinosaurs would still be alive and ruling Earth
test: Danylo's cousin: «history only goes up, every age is better, facts». Cap? | Cap; progress can go backwards in some places for a while | No cap, every single age is always better than the one before | No cap, nothing ever really changes from one age to another | Cap; every age is always worse than the one that came before
test: Finn guesses a billion people lived when the first farms began. Better guess? | A few million, about one big city today | Eight billion, about the same as there are now | Exactly one billion, so Finn is right | Only about fifty people in the whole world
test: Aisha's dad says people first talked «before WhatsApp». What is the honest answer? | Nobody knows exactly; spoken words leave no bones or stones | Exactly in 3000 BC, as the Egyptian records clearly show | When writing was invented, because speech came from letters | Only in the last 2,000 years, after the Roman Empire
exam: Identify the approximate period during which anatomically modern humans have existed. | Approximately three hundred thousand years | Approximately six thousand years | Approximately two thousand years | Approximately four and a half billion years
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origins of agriculture? | Agriculture arose independently in several regions of the world | Agriculture was devised in Mesopotamia and copied by all others | Agriculture preceded the earliest use of fire | Agriculture was introduced by the Roman Empire
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Stone, Bronze and Iron Ages. | They are classifications devised by historians, occurring at differing times in differing regions | They commenced simultaneously throughout the world, by the general agreement of all peoples | They were proclaimed by decree of ancient monarchs | They succeeded one another everywhere in the year one thousand
exam: Ascertain the approximate population of the world at the commencement of agriculture. | A few million persons | Approximately one billion persons | Approximately eight billion persons | Approximately fifty persons
